<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llandaff</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llandaff is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, on the River Taff, and the seat of the diocese of Llandaff. Its church claimed to be the see of the saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubricius">Dubricius</a>, Teilo and Euddogwy, and its 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-llandaff">Book of Llandaff</a> preserves charters of earlier kings of Glywysing and Gwent. The present cathedral was begun in 1120.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llandaff.jpg" alt="Llandaff" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dolica · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llandaff_Cathedral.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Llandaff lies in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a>, and its name joins the Welsh llan, church, with the name of the Taff. It probably became a place of Christian worship in the 6th century because it was the first firm ground north of the point where the Taff met the Bristol Channel, and because it stood at a river crossing on a north–south trade route. Romano-British ritual burials have been found under the present cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The date at which the cathedral moved to Llandaff is disputed, but parts of the fabric date from the 12th century, among them the Romanesque Urban Arch, named after Bishop Urban. The building has repeatedly been destroyed and restored: it suffered attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> bombing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was the second most damaged cathedral in the United Kingdom after Coventry. The architect George Pace restored it, and Jacob Epstein's aluminium figure of Christ in Majesty, made in 1954–5, hangs above the nave. The Bishop's Palace south of the cathedral was probably built in the late 13th century and abandoned after Glyndŵr damaged it in the 15th century; its gatehouse survives.</p><p>Because it was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat, Llandaff was informally called a city, though it never received a charter of incorporation or became a borough. By the 19th century it was described as reduced to a mere village of two short streets. Its ancient parish also took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, Ely, Fairwater and Gabalfa. With the growth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> coalfield and Cardiff Docks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> merchants such as the Insole family settled there, and on 9 November 1922 the county borough of Cardiff was extended to include it. On 14 July 1952 an RAF Vampire jet crashed into a building next to the Llandaff Hotel after a mid-air collision, killing a woman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Llandaff is the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Llandaff, whose diocese in the Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> covers the most populous area of the country. It had 8,997 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Broadcasting House, opened there in 1966, was the headquarters of BBC Cymru Wales until 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Llandaff” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom covered the fertile vale and uplands between the Tywi and the Wye, with churches such as <b>Llandaff</b> and Llancarfan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-morgannwg">Kingdom of Morgannwg</a></li></ul></section>
